# Live-input structure across model classes — appendix J.0 prior facts
# Registered: benchmarks/sota944_campaign_2026-08-03.md "REGISTERED APPENDIX J"
# Source: the bake_contrib TSVs committed 2026-08-04 (benchmarks/bake_contrib_*.tsv);
#         `dead` = the registered mean|Δ| < 1e-4 AND p95|Δ| < 1e-3 verdict,
#         frac_rows_nonzero_xt = fraction of corpus rows where the STANDARDIZED
#         post-transform input is nonzero (0.0 => the column is constant there).
# Derived by scripts/featsub/ analysis in this pass; no statistic recomputed.
#
# THE FINDING this table exists for: `n_dead_but_varying` is the honest measure
# of "inputs the model chose to ignore". The 944 MLP class ignores ZERO varying
# inputs — all 277 of its dead inputs are constant columns (216 structural
# zeros f156-371 + 61 never-populated/winsor-collapsed). B, the shipped default
# and the model nothing beats axis-by-axis under the balanced frame, ignores
# 277 of 372 VARYING inputs (74%). So the sparsity gap between the two classes
# is not a rounding difference in how "dead" is defined: one class learned a
# subset, the other kept everything it was given.
#
# Cross-model ranking agreement (same tool, same method, computed this pass):
#   * B's 46 live inputs inside the shared v1-basic block f0..f155 sit at a
#     MEDIAN rank of 338/944 in the 944 model's contribution order. The 944
#     top-64 captures 3 of them (7%); top-256 captures 22 (48%).
#   * SROCC(winner_dial's contribution rank, the 944 model's contribution rank)
#     over the 156 shared v1-basic inputs = 0.3588 (zensim-validate `panel`,
#     n=156, PLCC 0.4436, KROCC 0.2412).
# Two good models disagree substantially about which of the SAME inputs matter.
# That is the registered J.2 caveat, measured: a contribution ranking is a
# property of the model it was measured on, which is why Phase A alone can
# support "flat" or "degrades" strongly but supports "a subset is BETTER" only
# weakly, and why Phase B learns the subset instead of ranking it.
#
# DERIVED PREDICTION recorded before the K sweep was read (it follows from the
# J.1 mechanism, so it is a consistency gate, not a new hypothesis):
# K667 drops exactly the 277 columns that are constant ON THE TRAINING SET, and
# a constant column already standardizes to 0.0, so in the K944 baseline those
# weight rows receive zero loss-gradient and never touch the forward. K667 and
# K944 at the same seed should therefore agree very closely. They can still
# differ where a column is constant in TRAINING but varies in EVAL: 216 of the
# 277 are structural zeros (identically 0 in the 944 regime) and 24 more are
# winsored to [0,0] by the recipe's own transforms, but the remaining ~37 are
# merely never-populated in training, and K944's non-zero (init-drifted) rows
# would consume them at eval time while K667's zeroed rows cannot. Whatever the
# K667-vs-K944 delta turns out to be, it is that effect, and it is reported as
# measured rather than assumed.
